+\revredreplace{We propose \textbf{\samcgs{}}, a structure-aware Monte Carlo graph search method for extracting risk subgraphs from cyclic document regions.  \samcgs{} first finds \scc{} bottlenecks, then performs local window rollouts inside each \scc{}.  Instead of treating risk as intrinsic to a single node, it stores relation-first evidence, where a node becomes risky when earlier evidence makes its constraint, exception, or handoff incompatible with the graph context.  The final output is a dynamic core risk subgraph that can be inspected or passed upward as a collapsed evaluation point.}{We propose \textbf{\samcgs{}}, a structure-aware Monte Carlo graph search method that conducts local LLM rollouts within each \scc{}. A record\textquotesingle{}s priority reflects how its constraints, exceptions, or handoffs interact with evidence already observed. Relation-first memory preserves this context across rollouts and guides further inspection of implicated records and relations. A dynamic core allows earlier selections to be replaced as evidence accumulates, yielding a compact risk subgraph for inspection (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).}
 
 Our contributions are:
 \begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]
     \item \revredreplace{We formulate \emph{structural risk subgraph extraction}: given an \scc{} in a document graph, output a compact subgraph that retains the repair-relevant evidence endpoints, in place of single anomalous node.}{A formulation of \emph{structural risk subgraph extraction} that requires compact subgraphs to retain repair-relevant evidence from cyclic document regions.}
     \item \revredreplace{We introduce \samcgs{}, a structure-aware Monte Carlo graph search method that localizes \scc{}s, accumulates relation-level evidence through local rollouts, and distills persistent signals into a compact dynamic risk core.  Its rollout policy uses upper confidence bound (UCB) selection augmented with progressive relation priors, critical-pair revisiting, and evidence-gated core pruning, so additional search budget is routed toward repeatedly repair-relevant relations, not arbitrary path copies.}{\samcgs{}, an adaptive search method combining upper confidence bound (UCB) selection with progressive relation priors, critical-pair revisiting, and evidence-gated core pruning.}
-    \item \revredreplace{We build a frozen critical-stress benchmark from four authority-backed sources. Debian provides package-dependency graphs, SEC EX-21 provides subsidiary-disclosure graphs, the German Civil Code (BGB) provides statutory cross-reference graphs, and CUAD provides contract-clause graphs. The benchmark contains 320 model-case pairs evaluated with GPT-4o, DeepSeek-V3, Qwen2.5-72B, and Gemini 2.5 Pro. Across these settings, \samcgs{} improves \rootthree{}, \riskany{}, and \riskall{} over an oracle-risk full-\scc{} direct-subgraph baseline. It also makes the retention cost visible through an explicit compression metric.}{A frozen four-domain benchmark for comparing \samcgs{} with one-shot prompting and LEA across five LLMs, complemented by component ablations, measured inference costs, and expert auditing.}
